{"abstract":"Objective: To investigate the relative contribution of genetic and environmental components to subfertility. Design: Twin design using a quantitative genetic liability threshold model that splits the variation of subfertility into additive genetic effects, common environmental effects, and unique environmental effects. Setting: Not applicable. Patients: A total of 9053 Danish monozygotic and dizygotic same-sex twins aged 18+ years from nationwide twin surveys (twins born 1931−1976). Intervention: Not applicable. Main outcome measures: Time to pregnancy (TTP) restricted to first pregnancy as a binary outcome, with a cut-off point of 10 months. Results: Based on the Akaike information criterion, a model including additive genetic and unique environmental factors resulted in the best model fit. For females, the relative contribution of additive genetic factors to TTP was 28% (95% confidence interval [CI] 15%, 41%), whereas unique environmental factors explained 72% (95% CI 59%, 85%). For males, additive genetic factors explained 4% (95% CI 0%, 22%) of the variation in TTP, while unique environmental factors accounted for 96% (95% CI 78%, 100%). Results were overall similar for the crude model and consistent across surveys. Conclusion: Unique environmental factors explain most of the observed variation in subfertility, when measured as waiting time to pregnancy.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Ahrenfeldt, Linda Juel","Möller, Sören","Wensink, Maarten Jan","Jensen, Tina Kold","Christensen, Kaare","Lindahl-Jacobsen, Rune"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-642641"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Ahrenfeldt, Linda Juel"],"keyword":["Heritability","Subfertility","Twins"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"heritability of subfertility among danish twins","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/172008663/Heritability_of_subfertility.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["We wish to thank the twins for their participation in the MADT and the Omnibus."],"id":"S01-000.00-642641","identifier":[{"id":"32624213","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85087370799","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-f27c6714-b2aa-4c35-a7eb-c97f7aca04fe"],"keyword":["Time to pregnancy fecundity","heritability","subfertility","twins"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-455456F75CFBC9B0D65C486F1C6093E4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/172008663/Heritability_of_subfertility.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Public Health, Epidemiology, Biostatistics and Biodemography","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Public Health, The Danish Twin Registry"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Linda Juel","key":"ahrenfeldtpau","last":"Ahrenfeldt","locid":"286f9573-a0ee-4487-b04b-ed1e883a39f5","name":"Ahrenfeldt, Linda Juel","orcid":"0000-0002-5018-1642","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Sören","key":"möllerpau","last":"Möller","locid":"905018dc-c6c4-4a79-b50d-d0c08618e77a","name":"Möller, Sören","orcid":"0000-0003-0858-4269","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Maarten","key":"wensinkpau","last":"Wensink","locid":"bf6dc941-d180-493d-b3d7-0d17d15101f0","name":"Wensink, Maarten Jan","name_org":"Wensink, Maarten","orcid":"0000-0001-6518-1015","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Tina Kold","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"50e57e0b-3ce7-4b7d-9d24-125d84b7a62d","name":"Jensen, Tina Kold","orcid":"0000-0003-2311-5778","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Kaare","key":"christensenpau","last":"Christensen","locid":"35c0eca6-0f49-465b-9bbd-ce71bcf2c4ac","name":"Christensen, Kaare","orcid":"0000-0002-5429-5292","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rune","key":"lindahljacobsenpau","last":"Lindahl-Jacobsen","locid":"069d0e17-d8fd-42e8-a1bb-967bb3c54b74","name":"Lindahl-Jacobsen, Rune","orcid":"0000-0002-4622-9826","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["32624213"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.fertnstert.2020.03.014","issn":["00150282","15462501"],"issn_org":["00150282"],"issue":"3","journal":"Fertility and Sterility","pages":"618-627","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Fertility and Sterility","volume":"114"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85087370799"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-f27c6714-b2aa-4c35-a7eb-c97f7aca04fe"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Heritability of subfertility among Danish twins","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-642641","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Postprandial gut hormone responses change after Roux-en-Y gastric bypass (RYGB), and we investigated the impact of glucose, protein, and fat (with and without pancreas lipase inhibition) on plasma responses of gut and pancreas hormones, bile acids, and fibroblast growth factor 21 (FGF-21) after RYGB and in nonoperated control subjects. In a randomized, crossover study 10 RYGB operated and 8 healthy weight-matched control subjects were administered 4 different 4-h isocaloric (200 kcal) liquid meal tests containing >90 energy (E)% of either glucose, protein (whey protein), or fat (butter with and without orlistat). The primary outcome was glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) secretion (area under the curve above baseline). Secondary outcomes included responses of peptide YY (PYY), glucose- dependent insulinotropic polypeptide (GIP), cholecystokinin (CCK), glicentin, neurotensin, ghrelin, insulin, glucagon, bile acids, and FGF-21. In the RYGB group the responses of GLP-1, GIP, glicentin, FGF-21, and C-peptide were increased after glucose compared with the other meals. The neurotensin and bile acids responses were greater after fat, while the glucagon and CCK responses were greater after protein ingestion. Furthermore, compared with control subjects, RYGB subjects had greater responses of total PYY after glucose, glucagon after glucose and fat, glicentin after glucose and protein, and GLP-1 and neurotensin after all meals, while GIP and CCK responses were lower after fat. Ghrelin responses did not differ between meals or between groups. Orlistat reduced all hormone responses to fat ingestion, except for ghrelin in the RYGB group. In conclusion, after RYGB glucose is a more potent stimulator of most gut hormones, especially for the marked increased secretion of GLP-1 compared with fat and protein.","author_count":13,"contributors":["Jensen, Christian Zinck","Bojsen-Møller, Kirstine N","Svane, Maria S","Holst, Line M","Hermansen, Kjeld","Hartmann, Bolette","Wewer Albrechtsen, Nicolai J","Kuhre, Rune Ehrenreich","Kristiansen, Viggo B","Rehfeld, Jens Frederik","Clausen, Trine R","Holst, Jens J","Madsbad, Sten"],"cuid":["S02-091.01-666447"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Bile acids","Carbohydrate","FGF21","Gut hormones","Protein","RYGB"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-NOVO"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-NOVO","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"responses of gut and pancreatic hormones bile acids and fibroblast growth factor 000000000021 differ to glucose protein and fat ingestion after gastric bypass surgery","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85082145885","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-091.01-666447","identifier":[{"id":"32068442","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85082145885","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-ba7842b2-d786-4d85-9500-909256fc71e7","ku-50c85278-19bd-47b6-9ad7-8ec4b6c52ae3"],"keyword":["Bile acids","Carbohydrate","FGF21","Gut hormones","Protein","RYGB"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-8EE0BA5C7EE5ED2AE650D999D82F4B70"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Basic Metabolic Research"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU